The question: "Write a skeletal format of C programs." They list several options showing sections like data types, macros, function prototypes, etc. In C, typical skeleton:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <...>   // preprocessor directives
/* function prototypes */
int main(void) {
    // statements
    return 0;
}

/* other functions */

So the typical skeleton includes preprocessor directives (macros, includes), function prototypes (or global declarations), main, then other functions. Among options, which matches best? Option G: "[preprocessor directives] main ( ) { [program code] } [libraries]". However libraries usually come before, not after. Option B: "[function prototypes] [globals] main ( ) { [control structures] } [subroutines]". That seems plausible: prototypes, globals, main with control structures, then subroutines. Option A: "[data types] [macros] main ( ) { [execution flow] } [helper functions]". Not typical.

Option D: "[constant declarations] [externals] main ( ) { [program statements] } [additional functions]". Could be but externals maybe not.

Option H: "[variable declarations] [externals] main ( ) { [program conditions] } [additional functions]". Not typical.

I think the canonical skeleton: preprocessor directives, function prototypes, global variable declarations, main, other functions. None exactly list both prototypes and includes. Option B includes function prototypes and globals, then main then subroutines. That matches best.

Thus answer B.

We need to translate English: "Some firefighters rescue victims from danger."

Interpretation: There exists at least one firefighter who rescues at least one victim from (some) danger. It does not say every firefighter does it, nor that the same danger for all victims, just that for a given firefighter, there is at least one victim and at least one danger such that the firefighter rescues that victim from that danger. Also could be read as: For some firefighter, for every victim, there exists a danger ... but typical reading of "Some firefighters rescue victims from danger" is ambiguous because "victims" could be plural generic; but likely means "Some firefighters rescue victims from danger" meaning there is at least one firefighter who rescues some victims, i.e., there exists a firefighter who rescues at least one victim, and the rescue is from danger. So existential quantifier for firefighter, existential for victim, existential for danger, with relation Rxyz where x rescues y from z. Also need predicates: Fx firefighter, Vy victim, Dz danger. The relation Rxyz: x rescues y from z. So we need Fx ∧ ∃y (Vy ∧ ∃z (Dz ∧ Rxyz)). Also need outer existential for firefighter: ∃x (Fx ∧ ...). So the formula should be of form ∃x (Fx ∧ ∃y (Vy ∧ ∃z (Dz ∧ Rxyz))). Look at options.

Check each:

A) (∀x){Fx ⊃ (∃y)[Vy . (∃z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
This says every firefighter rescues some victim from some danger. Too strong (universal). Not correct.

B) (∃x){Fx • (∀y)[Dy ⊃ (∃z)(Vz • Rxyz)]}
Exists a firefighter such that for all y, if y is danger then there exists a victim rescued from that danger. That's weird ordering; also quantifiers reversed; not matching.

C) (∀x){Fx ⊃ (∃y)[Dy . (∃z)(Vz • Rxyz)]}
Every firefighter rescues some danger? Not correct.

D) (∃x){Fx • (∀y)[Vy ⊃ (∀z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
Exist firefighter such that for all victims, for all dangers, Dz -> Rxyz? Actually (∀z)(Dz • Rxyz) means for all z, Dz and Rxyz hold, i.e., all objects are dangers and are rescued? Not correct.

E) (∃x){Fx • (∀y)[Vy ⊃ (∃z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
Exist firefighter such that for every victim, there exists a danger from which the firefighter rescues that victim. This says the firefighter rescues all victims (maybe each from some danger). Not required.

F) (∀x){Fx ⊃ (∀y)[Vy ⊃ (∃z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
Every firefighter rescues every victim (some danger). Not correct.

G) (∃x){Fx • (∃y)[Dy • (∃z)(Vz • Rzxy)]}
Exist firefighter, exist danger, exist victim, but relation Rzxy where order mismatched: Rzxy means z rescues x from y? Actually Rxyz defined as x rescues y from z. So Rzxy would be z rescues x from y, not appropriate. Also quantifier order: first y is danger then z victim, reversed. Not correct.

H) (∃x){Fx • (∃y)[Vy • (∃z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
This matches: ∃x (Fx ∧ ∃y (Vy ∧ ∃z (Dz ∧ Rxyz))). Yes.

I) (∃x){Fx • (∃y)[Dy • (∃z)(Vz • Rxyz)]}
Exists firefighter, exists danger, exists victim, but order: Dy first then Vz, but still Rxyz where x firefighter, y danger? Actually inside they have Dy • (∃z)(Vz • Rxyz). Here y is danger, z is victim. Rxyz expects y = victim, z = danger. So mismatched.

J) (∃x){Fx • (∃y)(∃z)[(Vy • Dz) • Rxzy]}
Here R x z y? Actually Rxzy: x rescues z from y? The order: first argument x rescuer, second rescued, third danger. Rxzy would be x rescues z from y. Here they have Vy (victim) and Dz (danger) but the relation uses z as rescued (victim) and y as danger, but they also have Vy and Dz naming. However the conjunction (Vy • Dz) ties variable y to victim and z to danger, but then Rxzy uses x rescues z from y (so rescues danger from victim) wrong.

Thus correct translation is option H.

Check there is no other that matches. H is exactly the existential for firefighter, victim, danger with correct order.

Thus answer H.

We need to translate: "Some firefighters rescue victims from danger."

Interpretation: There exists at least one firefighter such that that firefighter rescues some victim from some danger. Could also mean: For some firefighter, for every victim, there exists a danger from which the firefighter rescues that victim? No, natural reading: Some firefighters (i.e., at least one) rescue victims (some victims) from danger (some danger). So existential quantifier over firefighters, existential over victims, existential over danger, with the rescue relation linking firefighter, victim, danger. Also need to ensure victim and danger are correctly typed.

Thus structure: ∃x (Fx ∧ ∃y (Vy ∧ ∃z (Dz ∧ R x y z))). Equivalent forms may reorder quantifiers, but must have existentials. Also need conjunctions inside.

