Exists firefighter such that for every victim, there exists a danger from which that firefighter rescues that victim. This says the firefighter rescues every victim (maybe different danger each). Not what "some firefighters rescue victims" (could be just some victims). So not correct.

F: (∀x){Fx ⊃ (∀y)[Vy ⊃ (∃z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
All firefighters rescue every victim (some danger). Too strong.

G: (∃x){Fx • (∃y)[Dy • (∃z)(Vz • Rzxy)]}
Here order of R arguments: Rzxy corresponds to rescues ? The predicate defined as Rxyz: x rescues y from z. So Rzxy would mean z rescues x from y, which mismatches. Also the inner conjunction uses Dy then Vz, but Vz is victim, Dz danger. Wrong.

H: (∃x){Fx • (∃y)[Vy • (∃z)(Dz • Rxyz)]}
Exists firefighter, exists victim, exists danger, with appropriate conjunctions. This matches.

I: (∃x){Fx • (∃y)[Dy • (∃z)(Vz • Rxyz)]}
Existence of danger then victim, but order inside doesn't matter; however the inner existential for y is Dy (danger), then inner existential for z is Vz (victim). But the rescue relation uses x y z where y is victim, z is danger. Here y is danger, z is victim, mismatch. So not correct.

J: (∃x){Fx • (∃y)(∃z)[(Vy • Dz) • Rxzy]}
Here rescue predicate is Rxzy: x rescues z from y? Actually Rxzy means first argument x rescues second argument z from third argument y. That would be x rescues z (victim) from y (danger). That matches if second argument is victim, third is danger. Here they have (Vy • Dz) conjunction, but order: Vy is victim, Dz danger. So Rxzy with y as danger, z as victim is reversed: Rxzy = x rescues y from z? Wait original definition: Rxyz: x rescues y from z. So first argument rescuer, second victim, third danger. Therefore Rxzy means x rescues z from y (since second arg is z, third y). So Rxzy would have second argument z (victim), third y (danger). In the formula they have (Vy • Dz) meaning y is victim, z danger, but then Rxzy would treat y as danger, z as victim, mismatch. So not correct.

Thus best translation is H.

